# apps/web — the manager/admin/locum dashboard.
#
# This app is a back-end-for-front-end (see src/lib/session.ts). Every API
# call is made from the Next.js SERVER — Server Components and Server
# Actions — never from the browser. There is exactly one env var because
# there is exactly one thing this app talks to over the network.

# The API's base URL. Deliberately NOT prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ tells Next.js to inline a value into the browser bundle. That
# would ship the backend's URL to every visitor's browser for no reason this
# app has a use for — nothing client-side calls the API, ever, by design —
# and it would be an open invitation for a future change to start fetching it
# from the browser, which is exactly the mistake session.ts's httpOnly
# cookies exist to prevent (a stolen access/refresh token via XSS).
#
# `api.ts` reads this with `import "server-only"` at the top of the file,
# which makes Next.js fail the build if any client component ever imports it
# — the same protection applies to this value by construction.
#
# Local dev: point at your local API (make up-native / pnpm --filter
# @locum/api start, default port 3000).
#
# Vercel (production/preview): the Railway API service's PUBLIC URL — e.g.
# https://locum-api-production.up.railway.app, or a custom domain once
# attached. NOT a *.railway.internal hostname — those only resolve inside
# Railway's own private network, and Vercel's functions run outside it.
API_URL=http://localhost:3000

# First admin. Server-only — never NEXT_PUBLIC_. Password must be 12+ chars.
# On Vercel: Settings → Environment Variables → Production, then redeploy.
# Then open /setup and click Create.
ADMIN_EMAIL=
ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Optional. Defaults to "Admin".
# ADMIN_FULL_NAME=
